GitHub Workflow → Newton's Laws of Motion PPT Project
Background
Subject: Physics (Newton's Laws of Motion unit)
Task: A group of 4 students collaborates to create a PPT on "Applications of Newton's Laws of Motion in Real Life," including force analysis, formula derivation, and real-world calculations.

Step 1: Create a Branch
Each person chooses a different real-life scenario and creates their own draft PPT.
They work independently without messing up the main version.

Specific actions:
The team leader creates a main framework PPT (title page + table of contents + review page of Newton's Three Laws).
Then:
- Student A works on car emergency braking (friction + acceleration)
- Student B works on elevator going up (apparent weight and weightlessness)
- Student C works on basketball projectile motion (parabolic trajectory)
- Student D works on object on an inclined plane (gravity components + acceleration)
Each person copies the main framework and renames it as "Name-Scenario-Draft.pptx".

Step 2: Make Changes & Commit
Each person saves a new version after finishing a step (force analysis, formula derivation, calculation).
Each save includes a short comment explaining what was done.

Specific actions:
Student A's save records:
- Day 1 → "Drew the force diagram for braking"
- Day 2 → "Derived a = μg and calculated braking distance"
- Day 3 → "Added real data (braking distance at 60 km/h)"
Student C's save records:
- Day 2 → "Wrote the projectile motion equations x(t) and y(t)"
- Day 4 → "Calculated max height and horizontal range"
Each save is labeled with a version number, e.g., "StudentA_Braking_v2.pptx".

Step 3: Push to GitHub
Upload the latest drafts to the team's shared cloud folder.
Everyone can access the latest work and files are backed up to prevent data loss.

Specific actions:
The team uses a Google Drive shared folder.
Each person uploads their latest version every day.
Student B's computer crashed once, but because they had uploaded recently, they only lost 15 minutes of work.

Step 4: Open a Pull Request (PR)
After finishing their section, a person notifies the team: "My part is done. Please review my force analysis and calculations."
This is a formal signal to start the quality check process.

Specific actions:
Student D finishes "Object on an Inclined Plane" and sends a message in the group chat:
"@Everyone My inclined plane section is done. It includes force diagrams, the acceleration derivation a = g(sinθ - μcosθ), and numerical calculations. Please check if the force arrow directions are correct."

Step 5: Review & Discuss
Teammates check the work, point out mistakes, and suggest improvements.
The author fixes the issues and uploads a new version.

Specific actions:
Student A checks Student D's PPT and says: "The friction direction is drawn wrong. When the object slides down, friction points upward along the incline, but you drew it pointing downward."
Student B suggests: "The calculation step is too jumpy. Please show the substitution process."
Student C points out: "The angle θ is marked as 30° in the diagram, but you used sin45° in the formula. They need to match."
Student D fixes everything and replies in the group: "All done! Thank you everyone."

Step 6: Merge
The leader combines all approved sections into one final PPT.
Fonts, diagram styles, and formula formats are unified.

Specific actions:
The leader opens "Final_Applications_of_Newton's_Laws.pptx".
They arrange the slides in this order: braking → elevator → projectile motion → inclined plane.
They unify all force diagrams: all arrows are red with clear labels.
They unify the formula font and add page numbers and a team member contribution page.

Step 7: Delete the Branch (Cleanup)
Move all drafts and old versions to an archive folder.
Only the final version stays in the main directory.

Specific actions:
The leader creates a folder called "Old Drafts" and moves all "v1/v2/v3" and "draft" files into it.
Only two files remain in the main directory:
① "Applications_of_Newton's_Laws_Group1_Final.pptx"
② "Presentation_Script.docx"
The leader sends a message: "Cleanup done! Please only use the Final version from now on."
